VERDICT: PASS

===========================================================================
DEVIATION FROM THE CASE -- READ BEFORE TRUSTING THE PASS
===========================================================================
The case specifies "LAG should straddle stack members". IT DID NOT HERE.
`sa1` was a SINGLE-MEMBER static aggregator built on port2.0.2 (stack member
2 only).

  WHAT IS THEREFORE PROVEN (the PASS above):
    an ACL binds to a static-aggregator interface (`interface sa1 /
    access-group <name>`) and correctly filters traffic transiting it.

  WHAT IS NOT PROVEN -- UNMEASURED:
    that the ACL is applied consistently across links terminating on
    DIFFERENT stack members. A one-legged LAG cannot distinguish "the ACL is
    programmed into every member's hardware" from "the ACL is programmed only
    into the member holding the link" -- which is plausibly the whole reason
    these four cases specify a straddling LAG.
    The case's "Test after stack failover, rejoin, reboot" is likewise NOT
    covered.

  WHY: each IE520-28GSX member has exactly ONE usable copper port
  (portN.0.1/3/4 are empty SFP cages). The only straddling pair that ever
  existed was port1.0.2 + port4.0.2 to the AR4050S, and port1.0.2 was
  recabled to the x230 on 2026-09-21. Building a straddling LAG needs a
  physical recable, which is with Terrence as a bench-access item.
